const items = [
  {
    id:       'aerin-bio-case-study',
    category: 'CASE STUDY',
    title:    'Aerin Bio: A Take-Home Brief, Treated Like a Real Launch',
    meta:     'Next.js · Design-to-build in one pass',
    body: [
      'Aerin Bio started as a take-home assignment for a creative frontend developer role — build a landing page for a fictional biotech company. The scope was small on paper; the execution wasn\'t treated that way.',
      'The brief needed a single idea to carry the whole page: "medicine that breathes with you," inhaled biologics delivered as a mist rather than through the bloodstream. That idea became the literal mechanic of the hero — the word "Aerin" rendered as an SVG mask that reveals a real photograph beneath it as you scroll, until the letters dissolve into the image.',
      'Everything downstream of that decision followed the same discipline: a cursor-reactive Canvas 2D smoke layer that only spawns particles on movement and stays blank at rest, GSAP-driven scroll-scrubbed sequences, a monochrome-plus-one-accent design system, and full `prefers-reduced-motion` support so the motion-heavy page degrades gracefully rather than breaking for users who need it to.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Brief', 'The Core Idea', 'The Execution'],
  },
  {
    id:       'fish-pond-case-study',
    category: 'CASE STUDY',
    title:    'Fish Pond: Comparing Three Ways to Make a Fish Decide',
    meta:     'Next.js + Three.js + ONNX · In development',
    body: [
      'Fish Pond set out to answer a specific question: what does it actually look like, side by side, to make a simulated creature decide what to do via reflex rules, probabilistic scripting, and a real on-device model?',
      'Cognitive Arena and the Visual Telemetry Benchmark answer that with no AI model at all — pure scripted behaviour, deliberately, so there\'s a clean control case to compare against. The Underwater scene is the other half: a goldfish environment with Triforge-authored materials, where an on-device ONNX model called Laya-AI makes the actual behavioural decisions while the renderer just executes them.',
      'The project is explicit about where it stands: Laya-AI is a general text classifier repurposed for this task, not a fish-specific model, performance hasn\'t been measured on real GPUs yet, and System 2 reasoning for the underwater fish is still on the roadmap. Documenting the gaps is as much a part of the case study as the parts that work.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Question', 'Three Approaches', 'What\'s Still Open'],
  },
  {
    id:       'inkwake-case-study',
    category: 'CASE STUDY',
    title:    'Inkwake: Proving a Framework Claim Instead of Asserting It',
    meta:     'TypeScript · Lighthouse, 3 frameworks, 1 page',
    body: [
      'Building Strata raises an obvious question: does a JIT CSS framework you wrote yourself actually ship less CSS than Tailwind or Bootstrap, or does it just feel that way? Inkwake exists to answer that with evidence rather than assertion.',
      'The same anime-themed e-commerce landing page — mega menu, master footer, 19 content sections, three-theme toggle — gets built three times, once per framework, with markup intent, copy, and business logic held identical across all three. Only the CSS framework and its native JS idiom change.',
      'Each build deploys live and runs through Lighthouse under mobile and desktop presets, ten isolated runs per app per mode, with medians reported alongside means to filter out CPU-contention noise from parallel runs — the same rigor a real performance audit would demand, applied to my own tool.',
    ],
    topics: ['Why It Exists', 'What\'s Held Constant', 'The Methodology'],
  },
  {
    id:       'results',
    category: 'SUMMARY',
    title:    'What These Three Have in Common',
    meta:     'The thread across the case studies',
    body: [
      'Different briefs, different stacks, same underlying habit: pick one idea or one question, and build the entire project in service of answering it rather than listing features. Aerin Bio had a design idea it had to physically embody. Fish Pond had a comparison it had to run fairly. Inkwake had a claim it had to prove or disprove.',
      'None of these are commercial client engagements — there are no clients yet, and no NDA to work around. Everything here is public: the code, the commit history, and the repo issues where the rough edges are tracked openly instead of smoothed over for a case-study page.',
      'Full repos, READMEs, and live demos are linked from Earth and Venus. This page is the "why it was built this way" layer on top of them.',
    ],
    topics: ['The Common Thread', 'No NDA Needed', 'Where to Go Next'],
  },
];
